theory.intro:
You can graph $y = mx + c$ without a table of values. Plot the $y$-intercept, use the gradient as a rise-over-run step to reach a second point, then rule the line through both.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!45,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-1.6,-3.4) -- (2.8,3.2);
\draw[dashed] (0,-1) -- (2,-1) node[midway,below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {run};
\draw[dashed] (2,-1) -- (2,2) node[midway,right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {rise};
\fill (0,-1) circle (2pt);
\fill (2,2) circle (2pt);
\node[above left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.2,-0.95) {$(0,c)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
